
Adaptive Hybrid AXI–APB Memory Interface for Efficient CPU–SRAM Communication

Problem Statement: 
Many modern SoC systems rely on high-speed communication protocols for all memory transactions, even when 
the workload is minimal. This leads to inefficient use of hardware resources and increased dynamic power 
consumption. An optimized memory interface is required that can adapt between high-performance communication
 and low-power communication depending on the workload.

This project focuses on the design and functional verification of a hybrid memory interface between CPU and RAM 
using AXI and APB protocols. The CPU behaviour is modelled using System Verilog classes, where address and data
are randomized through constrained randomization to emulate real-time traffic patterns. A Traffic Analyzer is 
built which monitors the transactions and classifies them into Sequential Burst and Random Burst.

Based on this analysis, AXI protocol is selected for high-speed burst transfers, while APB is used for low-power
and control-oriented operations. The SRAM is implemented as a DUT module.
Randomized transactions are driven through the selected protocol to perform write and read operations, 
and the results are verified using a scoreboard by comparing the expected and actual data based on the memory address.
The verification environment validates both protocol functionality, protocol switching, and SRAM correctness. 
The proposed hybrid approach aims to improve power and performance trade-offs compared to single-protocol interfaces,
 making it suitable for low-power and high-performance SoC designs.

Result of our Project :
•	A comparative analysis was  performed between the AXI Interface and the AXI-APB Hybrid Interface,
and their power consumption reports were evaluated to demonstrate the efficiency and significance of the proposed design.
